Subject: add interrupt checks to the string library C loops

The string library is where sandboxed Lua can spend unbounded time
inside C without executing a single VM instruction, which makes it
invisible to the count hook the watchdog relies on. Each of these
loops now polls the interrupt flag:

  match()          at the 'init' label, so both plain entry and every
                   'goto init' tail-recursion round are covered
  max_expand()     both loops (the singlematch scan and the backtrack)
  min_expand()     the for(;;) backtracking loop
  lmemfind()       the plain-search loop reached from string.find with
                   plain=true or a pattern with no specials; worst case
                   is O(l1 * l2), e.g. finding ('a'):rep(1e6) .. 'b'
                   inside ('a'):rep(1e7). Guarded by an extra lua_State
                   argument, hence the duplicated declaration
  str_find_aux()   the do/while that slides the anchor
  gmatch_aux()     the for loop over start positions
  str_gsub()       the while over replacements
  str_rep()        the memcpy loop, once per LUAEXT_REP_CHUNK (64KiB)
                   rather than per iteration, since that loop is pure
                   memory traffic

Upstream's own 'matchdepth' counter (200) already caps recursion depth,
so no hand-rolled recursion limit is added.

